# Implementation Plan - Null.sys Hijack Driver & Mapper

This project aims to create a kernel driver that, when manually mapped, hijacks the `Null.sys` driver by replacing its dispatch table. This allows the driver to execute code whenever `Null.sys` is called (e.g., via IOCTLs).

## User Review Required

> [!WARNING]
> Manual mapping into kernel memory requires a way to bypass Driver Signature Enforcement (DSE). This plan assumes the use of a vulnerable driver or a system with DSE disabled for testing.
> Hijacking system drivers can cause system instability or Blue Screen of Death (BSOD) if not handled carefully.

## Proposed Changes

### Kernel Driver (`Driver/`)
The driver will be designed to be "Reflective" (manual map compatible).

#### [NEW] [main.c](file:///c:/Users/clem/Documents/Mapper/Driver/main.c)
- `DriverEntry`: Entry point called by the mapper. It will not use standard Windows loading.
- `HijackNull`: 
    - Use `IoGetDeviceObjectPointer` or `ObReferenceObjectByName` to get `\Driver\Null`.
    - Access the `DRIVER_OBJECT` structure.
    - Backup `MajorFunction[IRP_MJ_DEVICE_CONTROL]`.
    - Set `MajorFunction[IRP_MJ_DEVICE_CONTROL]` to our `HookedDeviceControl`.
- `HookedDeviceControl`: 
    - Check the `IoControlCode`.
    - If it matches our custom code, perform the desired action.
    - Otherwise, call the original `Null.sys` handler.

### Manual Mapper (`Mapper/`)
A user-mode application to load the driver.

#### [NEW] [mapper.cpp](file:///c:/Users/clem/Documents/Mapper/Mapper/mapper.cpp)
- `main`: Orchestrates the mapping.
- `MapDriver`: 
    - Reads the driver binary.
    - Allocates kernel memory (using a vulnerable driver driver or similar mechanism).
    - Resolves relocations.
    - Resolves imports (mapping `ntoskrnl.exe` exports).
    - Calls the driver's entry point.

> [!NOTE]
> For the mapper, I will provide a structure that uses a placeholder for the kernel R/W primitive, as the specific method of kernel access (Vulnerable Driver, CVE, etc.) depends on the user's environment.

## Verification Plan

### Automated Tests
- None (Kernel development requires manual testing and debugging).

### Manual Verification
1. Load the mapper.
2. Mapper loads the driver into kernel memory.
3. Driver hijacks `Null.sys`.
4. Send an IOCTL to `\\.\Null` using a small testing utility or `DeviceIoControl`.
5. Check `DbgView` for logs from the hijacked driver.
